examples.question_text:
The graph below has edges $PQ$, $QR$, $RS$, $SP$, $PR$ and $QS$. The crossing is not a vertex. Explain what planar means, then redraw this graph without crossings and state whether it is planar.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=10mm,draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (P) at (0,0);
\coordinate (Q) at (3,0);
\coordinate (R) at (3,3);
\coordinate (S) at (0,3);
\draw (P)--(Q)--(R)--(S)--(P);
\draw (P)--(R);
\draw (Q)--(S);
\foreach \v in {P,Q,R,S}{\fill[black] (\v) circle (2pt);}
\path (P) node[below left] {$P$};
\path (Q) node[below right] {$Q$};
\path (R) node[above right] {$R$};
\path (S) node[above left] {$S$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
A graph is planar if its edges can be drawn without crossings, except where they meet at a shared vertex.

Place $S$ inside triangle $PQR$ and keep all six connections.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=10mm,draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (P) at (0,0);
\coordinate (Q) at (4,0);
\coordinate (R) at (2,3);
\coordinate (S) at (2,1);
\draw (P)--(Q)--(R)--(P);
\draw (P)--(S);
\draw (Q)--(S);
\draw (R)--(S);
\foreach \v in {P,Q,R,S}{\fill[black] (\v) circle (2pt);}
\path (P) node[below left] {$P$};
\path (Q) node[below right] {$Q$};
\path (R) node[above] {$R$};
\path (S) node[below] {$S$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The redraw keeps $PQ$, $QR$, $RS$, $SP$, $PR$ and $QS$, with no crossings. The graph is planar.

examples.question_text:
Count the faces in this crossing-free drawing. Explain how the loop at $L$ and the edge $KN$ affect your count.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=10mm,draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (J) at (0,0);
\coordinate (K) at (3.6,0);
\coordinate (L) at (3.6,2.8);
\coordinate (M) at (0,2.8);
\coordinate (N) at (5,-0.9);
\draw (J)--(K)--(L)--(M)--(J);
\draw (J)--(L);
\draw (K)--(N);
\draw (L) .. controls (2.4,4.5) and (4.8,4.5) .. (L);
\foreach \v in {J,K,L,M,N}{\fill[black] (\v) circle (2pt);}
\path (J) node[below left] {$J$};
\path (K) node[below left] {$K$};
\path (L) node[right] {$L$};
\path (M) node[above left] {$M$};
\path (N) node[below right] {$N$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
The diagonal $JL$ makes two enclosed regions inside the rectangle. The loop encloses a third region. The edge $KN$ ends at $N$ and encloses no region, so it adds no face.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=10mm,draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (J) at (0,0);
\coordinate (K) at (3.6,0);
\coordinate (L) at (3.6,2.8);
\coordinate (M) at (0,2.8);
\coordinate (N) at (5,-0.9);
\draw (J)--(K)--(L)--(M)--(J);
\draw (J)--(L);
\draw (K)--(N);
\draw (L) .. controls (2.4,4.5) and (4.8,4.5) .. (L);
\foreach \v in {J,K,L,M,N}{\fill[black] (\v) circle (2pt);}
\path (J) node[below left] {$J$};
\path (K) node[below left] {$K$};
\path (L) node[right] {$L$};
\path (M) node[above left] {$M$};
\path (N) node[below right] {$N$};
\path (2.5,0.8) node {$1$};
\path (1.1,2) node {$2$};
\path (3.6,3.6) node {$3$};
\path (5.3,2.5) node {$4$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Region $4$ is the unbounded outer face and must be included.

$\begin{aligned}&\mathrel{\phantom{=}}3+1\\&=4\end{aligned}$

The drawing has $4$ faces.

examples.question_text:
State whether each network is planar and give a reason.

(a) Five sensors are each connected directly to every other sensor.

(b) Three pumps are each connected directly to each of three tanks, as shown. All pipes must lie in one flat layer.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=10mm,draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (P1) at (0,2.6);
\coordinate (P2) at (2,2.6);
\coordinate (P3) at (4,2.6);
\coordinate (T1) at (0,0);
\coordinate (T2) at (2,0);
\coordinate (T3) at (4,0);
\foreach \p in {P1,P2,P3}{\foreach \t in {T1,T2,T3}{\draw (\p)--(\t);}}
\foreach \v in {P1,P2,P3,T1,T2,T3}{\fill[black] (\v) circle (2pt);}
\path (P1) node[above] {$P_1$};
\path (P2) node[above] {$P_2$};
\path (P3) node[above] {$P_3$};
\path (T1) node[below] {$T_1$};
\path (T2) node[below] {$T_2$};
\path (T3) node[below] {$T_3$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

For (b), state how the pipes could be installed if every connection must be retained.

The graph below is drawn without crossings.

(a) How many faces does it have?

(b) An edge $JL$ is added inside the rectangle without crossing any existing edge. How many faces does the new drawing have? Explain.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=10mm,draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (J) at (0,0);
\coordinate (K) at (4,0);
\coordinate (L) at (4,2.8);
\coordinate (M) at (0,2.8);
\coordinate (N) at (2,4.2);
\draw (J)--(K)--(L)--(M)--(J);
\draw (M)--(N)--(L);
\foreach \v in {J,K,L,M,N}{\fill[black] (\v) circle (2pt);}
\path (J) node[below left] {$J$};
\path (K) node[below right] {$K$};
\path (L) node[right] {$L$};
\path (M) node[left] {$M$};
\path (N) node[above] {$N$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
(a) The rectangle and triangle enclose two regions. Include the outer face.

$\begin{aligned}&\mathrel{\phantom{=}}2+1\\&=3\end{aligned}$

(b) The new edge $JL$ splits the rectangular face into two. The vertices $J$ and $L$ were already connected by the path $J$–$K$–$L$, so the face count increases by one.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=10mm,draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (J) at (0,0);
\coordinate (K) at (4,0);
\coordinate (L) at (4,2.8);
\coordinate (M) at (0,2.8);
\coordinate (N) at (2,4.2);
\draw (J)--(K)--(L)--(M)--(J);
\draw (M)--(N)--(L);
\draw (J)--(L);
\foreach \v in {J,K,L,M,N}{\fill[black] (\v) circle (2pt);}
\path (J) node[below left] {$J$};
\path (K) node[below right] {$K$};
\path (L) node[right] {$L$};
\path (M) node[left] {$M$};
\path (N) node[above] {$N$};
\path (2.8,0.8) node {$1$};
\path (1.2,2) node {$2$};
\path (2,3.3) node {$3$};
\path (5,3.5) node {$4$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$\begin{aligned}&\mathrel{\phantom{=}}3+1\\&=4\end{aligned}$

The new drawing has $4$ faces.
